    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/06/nike-streakfly-2-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: ZoomX cushioning is soft with a low 26/22 mm stack and high ground feel; heel feels slightly softer and forefoot slightly firmer, with protection best suited to short races.",
        "Responsiveness: The full-length carbon Flyplate plus ZoomX gives strong bounce and very snappy turnover, and the ride performs best at faster paces (about 200 m to 5K effort).",
        "Stability: Plate stiffness improves torsional control and forefoot rigidity, but rearfoot structure is minimal (small semi-rigid heel counter), so some runners needed tighter lacing for heel lock; the snug upper provides solid lateral hold in turns.",
        "Durability: Durability is expected to be below average overall; traction is reported as good on softer surfaces, but outsole/material longevity is not a strength."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/07/nike-streakfly-2-multi-tester-review-7.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: ZoomX is described as soft, but the ride is also harsh/aggressive versus typical road shoes; with a 27/23 mm low-stack, 4 mm drop setup, heel comfort is minimal and the shoe is best for forefoot strikers needing impact protection at speed.",
        "Responsiveness: The full-length carbon FlyPlate is a major upgrade over Streakfly 1, adding clear stiffness and snappier power transfer for quick turnover; it is highly race-oriented but still not as stiff as a Vaporfly.",
        "Stability: Stability improves through the plate stabilizing soft foam and a very secure upper lockdown/heel structure, yet the narrow, aggressively tapered platform and forward geometry make it less stable for heel strikers or less efficient runners.",
        "Durability: Traction is a standout with a phenomenally grippy outsole, but testers explicitly call out likely durability concerns given the ultra-light, minimal construction and weight-saving cutouts/material exposure."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/nike-streakfly-2-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning is intentionally low and race-focused (27 mm heel / 23 mm forefoot): ZoomX still feels soft, but overall underfoot feel is more road/forefoot-biased than plush, with enough impact protection for short hard efforts and very little heel comfort for heel strikers.",
        "Responsiveness is a standout: the stiff, aggressive carbon Flyplate plus ZoomX creates strong bounce/energy return and very snappy turnover, with an \"extra gear\" sensation at race pace.",
        "Stability is limited outside fast forefoot running: the narrow midfoot, deep cutout, and track-spike geometry feel unstable when walking or landing on the heel, though the snug upper provides secure lockdown at speed.",
        "Durability feedback is mostly outsole-focused: rubber is notably tacky/grippy (including wet runs), but the review does not provide long-term outsole wear or broader material-longevity results."
      ]
    }
  ],
